# Do not ship a balance change without a run

Reasoning about signs and coefficients regularly looks coherent and
is wrong. The only proof is numbers from the stand.

```powershell
# from the project root; close the Unity editor only for -Rebuild
powershell -Command ".\Tools\run-trial.ps1 -Rebuild -Duration 30 -Label check"
powershell -Command ".\Tools\run-trial.ps1 -Duration 20 -Label push_f -Extra @('-pushImpulse','18','-pushTime','5')"
powershell -Command ".\Tools\run-trial.ps1 -Duration 20 -Label oneg_l -Extra @('-standLeg','left','-standLegTime','5','-standLegHold','20')"
```

`-standLeg` — string `left`/`right`/`0`, not a number with a comma. `left` = support on the left (swing on the right). `-swingHip` / `-swingKnee` — swing flexion (hip negative, knee positive).

The project root is derived from the location of `Tools/run-trial.ps1`. Unity,
the build folder, and the summaries folder are set by `-UnityPath` / `-BuildFolder` /
`-TrialFolder` or `UNITY_EDITOR`, `TRIAL_BUILD_FOLDER`, `TRIAL_FOLDER`.
By default the build is `Build/Headless`, summaries are `Trials/Runs/<label>.json`
and the matching `.csv`. The script always passes `-fixedDelta 0.005`, unless
`Extra` sets its own step.

When changing only coefficients, do not rebuild the player: they
are overridden by arguments. If sources or the project's physics settings
do not match `build-manifest.json` next to the player, a run
without `-Rebuild` is refused. The summary writes the fingerprint from the build
manifest, not a fresh hash of the sources.

Script `Duration` defaults to **10 s**. Run a quiet stance for 30 s.

# Two baselines — do not mix them up

Acceptance of current changes is **`Trials/Baselines/stable-pd/`** (runs `s_on`,
`son_squat`, `son_pf*`, `son_pb*`) and the table in `current-front.mdc`.
The folder `Trials/Baselines/current/` is named that way historically and contains
**outdated** `curr_*` with the ostrich knee; do not read the name as “current”.
Old `damp_*` without provenance — `Trials/Baselines/legacy-no-provenance/`.

`buildManifestPath` and `commandArgs` may contain absolute paths:
they are not part of the fingerprint and will differ after the project is moved.

The table below is the **historical whole-torso baseline** (before the lumbar split).
Push thresholds from there must be restored, but do not compare stance numbers to it.

| Check | Historical whole torso |
|---|---|
| Quiet stance 30 s | does not fall, `bothFeetGroundedFraction` = 1 |
| Maximum torso tilt | about 6.36° (startup transient) |
| CoM offset RMS | about 0.0061 |
| Muscle saturation | 0 |
| Forward push | holds 22 N·s |
| Backward push | holds 26 N·s, falls at 28 |

# What to put in the report

`fell`, `survivedSeconds`, `maxAbsTorsoTilt`, `maxAbsPelvisTilt`,
`maxAbsLumbarAngle`, `pelvisDrop`, `rmsComOffset`,
`muscleSaturationFraction`. For stability changes — also push thresholds in
both directions.

`fell` by itself guarantees nothing: it only counts CoM offset from
the midpoint of the feet and does not see sinking. In run `pelv_p3` it has `fell: false` and
a full 30 seconds, while `pelvisDrop` there is 0.8385 — the human folded to the ground, but
CoM stayed over the feet. In quiet stance `pelvisDrop` holds 0.0017, in
a squat `crouch 1` — 0.0685. Anything noticeably larger without a requested
squat is a fall, however the flag looks.

# Metric definitions

- `fell` — `true` if the balance automaton became
  `Falling` on at least one step (`|CoM offset| > fallCoMOffset`, default 0.30 m).
- `survivedSeconds` — time of the first entry into `Falling`, otherwise the actual
  simulated time (slightly more than the requested `Duration` because of the step).
- `muscleSaturationFraction` — fraction of steps where the maximum activation
  of **hips and knees only** ≥ 0.999. Ankle, lumbar, neck, and arms
  are **not included** in saturation.
- `bothFeetGroundedFraction` — fraction of steps where both feet are on the ground.
- `swingFootGroundedFraction` / `stanceFootGroundedFraction` — in the
  `-standLeg` window (from `standLegTime` to release): fraction of steps where swing
  or stance touches the ground. Without a one-leg probe both are zeros.
- `recoverySeconds` — after a push: `lastUnsettledTime − pushTime`, where
  “not settled” means `|CoM offset| > 0.01` m. If there was no push or
  the offset never left 1 cm — 0. This is the duration of the last
  excursion, not “time to first settling”.
- Push: impulse on `Torso` along +X. Plus — forward, minus — backward.
  Default time is 5 s (`-pushTime`).

# Play Mode and trial are different modes

With `humanCount = 1` `GameProcess` sets `frictionMultiplier = 0.75`
(middle of the 0.5…1.0 range). The stand by default gives `-friction 1`.
Do not compare Play Mode numbers to the baseline. `GameProcess` was not
touched in this task.

# Experiment layer

`HeadlessTrial`, `TrialRecorder`, `HeadlessBuild` plus `GlobalHumanLogger`.
Sensors still only read; torques are created by `Muscle` and `JointFriction`.

# A comma in an argument silently cancels the change

`GetFloatArg` parses numbers in invariant culture. On a Russian locale
PowerShell formats `0.0154` as `0,0154`, parsing fails, and the stand
**silently takes the default**. The run finishes successfully and looks
valid, only the human in it is not the one you specified. That is how
three runs in the `visc_*` series were already lost.

Pass a number in `-Extra` as a string with a dot and do not build it through
interpolation from a `double`. Check not the intent, but the fact: the value must
match the `applied` block of the summary. That is exactly why it is written.

The stand does not yet fail on an unparsed argument, it only writes a warning to the log,
which nobody reads in batchmode. This is a defect of the experiment layer: parsing
must refuse as strictly as the provenance check.

# A repeat with the same label checks nothing

**Two feet, symmetric stance** — bitwise: `det_reg_a` / `det_reg_b` CSV
matched by hash on a build with `ConfigureDeterministicPhysics`.

**One foot** — the outcome is stable (`fell`, `pelvisDrop`, `stanceFoot`), but the CSV and
part of the summary diverge: first mismatch at ~5.06 s (`standLeg` turns on),
then the trajectory drifts. `swingFoot` in the `det_r*` series gives a band of ~0.09…0.18,
not a single number. A repeat for the one-leg probe is a check of **acceptance criteria**,
not bitwise CSV.

Before the 28.08 fix, one exe on right stance gave a **binary** flip: fell at 8 s
or 20 s on the same args (`oneg_r_a/b/c`). Root cause — PhysX 2D contact order
without `useConsistencySorting` and without a fixed FixedUpdate order.
Fix: `HeadlessTrial.ConfigureDeterministicPhysics()` (before and after scene
assembly), `Physics2DSettings.useConsistencySorting`, `[DefaultExecutionOrder]`
sensor −100 / control 0 / muscles +50.

For **two feet**, two runs with the same arguments give bitwise-equal
summaries. The push threshold is an exact number, not a statistic.

Margin of safety is checked by shifting a parameter, not by repeating. The cheapest
and meanest is `-activationSpeed`: at 40 and 80 instead of 20 the lumbar controller
broke quiet stance, which a repeat would never have shown.

The former note “24 N·s on the edge: one run held, the repeat fell” was
a consequence of different builds or different arguments, not physics scatter.

# Noise threshold: while the torso jitters, tenths of a degree mean nothing

Determinism holds only for the same code. An algebraically
neutral rewrite of a formula changes the order of floating-point operations,
and on a limit cycle that difference grows into a measurable one: replacing
`DeltaAngle(tilt, target)` with `tilt - target` shifted torso tilt
from 3.07° to 3.48° at the same RMS 0.0060.

Hence two conclusions. A difference of a few tenths of a degree cannot be taken as
the result of a change — that is noise. And one must look at a quantity that changes
by a factor: `rmsTorsoAngVel` in the same sweep went 55 → 74 → 124 → 164, that is
the signal.

Do not replace `DeltaAngle` with subtraction: on a fall the tilt goes past ±90°,
and the angle wrap is needed in substance.

# A push threshold is comparable only inside one body

The impulse is applied to `Torso`, so the threshold depends on the mass of that
segment. When the torso went from a rigid 30.4 kg to a compliant 22.6 kg on the lumbar
joint, the same 22 N·s started giving the chest more velocity and going into its
rotation — the test hardened by itself, without the controller getting worse.

When changing masses or torso connectivity, do not compare thresholds directly to the previous
baseline: first say what changed at the point of application.

# Body geometry pulls spawn height with it

Feet must touch the ground at y = −2.0, otherwise the human starts in the air
or in the ground. When changing segment sizes, recompute the spawn point in
`GameProcess` and `HeadlessTrial`:

`y0 = -2.0 + ((pelvisSize.y + torsoSize.y) / 2 + thighSize.y + shinSize.y + footSize.y)`

Currently `(0.16 + 0.34) / 2 + 0.43 + 0.43 + 0.07 = 1.18`, that is
`-2.0 + 1.18 = -0.82`.
